## 1. Frequency → Note Mapping (12-TET, A4 = 440)

### 1.1 Formulas
- Continuous pitch (MIDI-real): `m = 69 + 12·log2(f / 440)`
- Nearest semitone: `m₀ = round(m)`
- Cents deviation: `cents = 100·(m − m₀)`, range **[−50, +50) ¢**; sign convention: **+ = sharp of the named note, − = flat**.
- Equivalent general form: `cents(f, f_ref) = 1200·log2(f / f_ref)`.
- Note name: `NOTE_NAMES[m₀ mod 12]` with `['C','C#','D','D#','E','F','F#','G','G#','A','A#','B']`; **octave = floor(m₀/12) − 1** (scientific pitch notation; C4 = middle C = MIDI 60 ≈ 261.63 Hz).
- Wavelength in air (contextual): `λ = 343 / f` metres.

### 1.2 Perceptual range labels (mandatory)
Every frequency row carries a band label:

| Band | Range | Label string | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Infrasonic | f < 20 Hz | `INFRASONIC` | Note name still shown (allowed) but flagged; perceived as rhythm/pressure, not pitch. MIDI number may be negative — that is fine. |
| Low sub-pitch | 20–30 Hz | `SUB-PITCH EDGE` | Pitch perception unreliable below ~30 Hz; note name is nominal. |
| Audible pitch | 30 Hz – 4 kHz | `PITCH` | Full note + cents are perceptually meaningful. |
| High treble | 4–16 kHz | `HIGH (pitch weakens)` | Pitch discrimination degrades >4–5 kHz; cents lose perceptual meaning above ~8 kHz. |
| Ultrasonic edge | 16–20 kHz | `ULTRASONIC (borderline)` | Audible only to some listeners. |
| Ultrasonic | f > 20 kHz | `ULTRASONIC` | Not audible; note name is mathematical only. |

