## 2. Difference Vibrations (Beats & Ratio Analysis)

### 2.1 Definitions
For two decoded frequencies f₁, f₂ (convention: f_lo = min, f_hi = max):
- **Beat / difference frequency:** `Δf = |f₁ − f₂|` Hz.
- **Frequency ratio:** `r = f_hi / f_lo` (≥ 1).
- **Nearest just interval:** minimize `|cents_error|` over the JI table below, where `cents_error = 1200·log2(r / (p/q))`.
- **Octave reduction (optional second column):** reduce r into [1, 2) by dividing by 2^k before matching, to catch compound intervals (e.g., a tenth → major third). Report reduced ratio as e.g. `3.01 ≈ 2×3:2`.

### 2.2 Beat-rate perception categories (Δf)

| Δf range | Category label | Perceptual description |
|---|---|---|
| Δf = 0 | `UNISON (exact)` | No beating; infinite-sustain unison. Flag as a special case (see §5). |
| 0 < Δf < 0.5 Hz | `DRIFT` | Inaudible as beat; slow phase drift / very slow swell (period > 2 s). |
| 0.5 – 4 Hz | `SLOW BEAT` | Audible slow amplitude pulsing (tuning beats). |
| 4 – 20 Hz | `ROUGHNESS` | Roughness / tremolo-rate modulation; maximum roughness ≈ 15–20 Hz region (critical-band dependent; at low absolute frequencies the roughness peak shifts lower, ~70–100 Hz Δf near 1–2 kHz carriers, but as an absolute Δf band label we use 4–20 Hz). |
| 20 – ~40 Hz | `TRANSITION` | Beats blur into a tonal residue; borderline. |
| Δf > ~40 Hz | `DIFFERENCE TONE` | Perceived as a separate (combination/difference) tone, not a beat — if Δf itself is ≥ ~20–30 Hz and audible. **Do not call it a beat.** Also evaluate Δf itself as a pitch (run §1 mapping on Δf). |

*Caveat to print in footnote:* roughness depends on absolute frequency and critical bandwidth (max roughness ≈ ¼ of the critical bandwidth); the absolute-Δf bands above are a simplified presentation convention.

